    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our lineup architecture is heavily weighted toward stolen base upside to maintain our categorical advantage. **Justin Crawford**, **Masyn Winn**, and **Daulton Varsho** lead the charge, providing the high-floor speed production required to secure the SB category. While **Jonathan Aranda** sits at a marginal 46.15 score due to a daunting matchup against Jacob Misiorowski (2.35 xERA), he remains in the lineup based on his projected #2 spot in the order, which allows him to maximize high-leverage plate appearances against a pitcher who, despite his elite metrics, may be prone to fatigue. We are monitoring the lineup for **Gleyber Torres** and **JJ Bleday** closely; both carry significant platoon edges today that, if confirmed against right-handed starters, will stabilize our power floor in the middle innings.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Zack Gelof** versus **Tyler Freeman**. **Gelof** ultimately stayed on the bench because his BvP profile against today's projected arm suggested too high a variance in strikeout rate, whereas **Freeman** offers a more disciplined plate approach that aligns with our goal of protecting our batting average. We have opted to rest our heavy hitters\u2014**Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Bryce Harper**, **Cal Raleigh**, **Lawrence Butler**, **Yainer Diaz**, **Will Smith**, **Matt Chapman**, **Luis Arraez**, **Dylan Beavers**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, **Brendan Donovan**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, **Gavin Sheets**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Braden Montgomery**\u2014as their schedules align with our game-cap management strategy for this period. Proceed with caution on **Jonathan Aranda**; if official lineups move him down the order, his score will drop below our acceptable threshold, necessitating a late-swap to protect our overall daily efficiency.",
